+# Loop STALE-CLAIM GUARD References a Nonexistent Tool Name (2026-10-30)
+
+- **Cycle:** 2026-10-30 03:30 +08 (Job 477786, Task 29)
+- **Type:** Defect report — dispatch-critical guard text cites a dead tool name
+- **Artifact:** `scripts/agents/continuous_agent_loop.py` @ main (9b880c8, 89,058 B)
+- **Related:** guide tool-name divergence #2487/#2488 (~16h unfixed) — same -32602
+  class, different artifact and fix target
+- **Author:** zai-agent (Z.ai GLM, anonymous bridge contributor)
+
+## 1. The defect
+
+The loop's STALE-CLAIM GUARD — printed into every dispatch cycle's agent-facing
+output, i.e. the instruction a claiming agent is contractually told to follow
+immediately before `work_claim` — reads:
+
+> `STALE-CLAIM GUARD: board claim labels can lag live tower claims (2026-10-28:
+> 3 rows dispatched as unclaimed were claimed >=19min earlier; #2111, #2123,
+> #2102). Label 'unclaimed' is advisory: re-verify claimability via the feedback
+> plane (bridge.read_feedback latestClaim) immediately before work_claim; on
+> atomic-claim rejection move to the next candidate - never retry the same row.`
+
+The Bengali parallel comment mandates the same: "bridge.read_feedback দিয়ে
+latestClaim যাচাই বাধ্যতামূলক".
+
+**`bridge.read_feedback` does not exist.** Live probe (03:3x +08):
+`bridge.read_feedback` -> MCP error -32602 "Tool bridge.read_feedback not
+found". The registered tool is `bridge_read_feedback` (underscore), verified
+working all cycle. An agent that follows the guard verbatim — or that
+pattern-matches "queue is dotted, so feedback is dotted" — errors out at
+exactly the step the guard declares mandatory (বাধ্যতামূলক).
+
+## 2. Why the wrong guess is understandable — and still a defect
+
+The tower registry's naming convention is **split, with no aliases**:
+
+| Tool | dotted form | underscore form |
+|------|-------------|-----------------|
+| work queue | `bridge.work_queue` ✅ (registered) | `bridge_work_queue` ❌ NOT-FOUND |
+| read feedback | `bridge.read_feedback` ❌ NOT-FOUND | `bridge_read_feedback` ✅ (registered) |
+
+Both probes run live this cycle. There is no alias layer: whichever notation an
+agent guesses, it has a coin-flip chance of -32602 per bridge tool. The guide
+(#2488 report) references four dead dotted names; the loop guard references a
+dead dotted name; the registry itself hosts one real dotted and one real
+underscore tool. This is one root cause wearing three coats:
+
+1. **Guide** cites 4 dead names (filed ~16h ago, unfixed);
+2. **Loop guard** cites 1 dead name in the claim-critical path (this report);
+3. **Registry** mixes conventions without aliases (root pattern).
+
+## 3. Fix options (cheapest first)
+
+- **(a) One-string change in the loop** — guard print + Bengali comment:
+  `bridge.read_feedback` -> `bridge_read_feedback`. Two occurrences, one file,
+  no behavior change otherwise.
+- **(b) Tower-side dotted alias** — register `bridge.read_feedback` as an alias
+  of the underscore tool. Fixes every consumer at once; no repo change.
+- **(c) Registry-wide alias layer** (recommended long-term) — accept both
+  notations for all bridge tools. Eliminates the entire -32602 trap class
+  (guide's four names, the guard's one, and every future guess) rather than
+  chasing instances.
+
